    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "What is said to arise, on the wearing away of impurity, and to continue up to discriminative discernment."
      },
      "id": "pmc:LightOfKnowledge",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Leuchten der Erkenntnis",
        "en": "The light of knowledge"
      },
      "source": "Patañjali, Yogasūtra II.28",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:PatanjalaYoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Retention held at will without the filling and the emptying that ordinarily frame it. The manual calls it the accomplished form of the practice and says nothing in the three worlds is beyond one who holds it."
      },
      "id": "pmc:KevalaKumbhaka",
      "label": {
        "de": "Alleiniges Anhalten",
        "en": "Retention alone"
      },
      "source": "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.71-74 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hathayoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "An examination of the claim that the tummo practice produces inner heat, taken up because this is the one pairing in the collection whose capacity has an outward side that instruments reach."
      },
      "id": "pmc:TummoHeatExamination",
      "label": {
        "de": "Prüfung der Tummo-Hitze-Behauptung",
        "en": "Examination of the tummo heat claim"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "What the measurements bear on is the somatic claim and nothing beyond it. Reported temperature rises are consistent with the practice producing heat; they say nothing about the cosmology the practice is embedded in, and the node must not be read as though they did. The reported effect also depends on the forceful-breath component together with the visualisation, which means the claim that is actually supported is narrower than the one the tradition makes."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:protocolledPractice"
      },
      "pm:baseRate": "Temperature change under comparable breathing effort without the practice, in matched participants.",
      "pm:evidenceFrom": [
        "H. Benson et al., Body temperature changes during the practice of g-tummo yoga, Nature 295 (1982), 234-236",
        "M. Kozhevnikov et al., Neurocognitive and somatic components of temperature increases during g-tummo meditation, PLoS ONE 8(3) (2013), e58244, doi:10.1371/journal.pone.0058244"
      ],
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:casesWithDeviation"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "H. Benson and colleagues; M. Kozhevnikov and colleagues",
      "pm:falsifiedBy": "Practitioners of the forceful-breath tummo method show no rise in measured body temperature beyond that produced by the breathing alone in matched non-practitioners.",
      "pm:protocolUpdated": "2026-08-28",
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:TummoYieldsInnerHeat"
      },
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A Watcher named in the Aramaic-Greek teaching list as the one who disclosed the signs of the shooting stars. The position he occupies is held by Ezeqeel in the Ethiopic; no identification of the two is asserted here."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Ziqel",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ziqel",
        "en": "Ziqel"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Aramaic/Greek reconstruction; Nickelsburg 2001)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:ConcentrationPresupposesVirtue",
      "label": {
        "de": "Sammlung setzt Sittlichkeit voraus",
        "en": "Concentration presupposes virtue"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The handbook is built as a commentary on a canonical verse in which the one established in virtue develops mind and understanding, and it glosses the order accordingly."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:Samadhi"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:Sila"
      },
      "source": [
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga I.1 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga I.7-I.8 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Theravada"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:SixteenStepsOfBreathing",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die sechzehn Schritte der Atemachtsamkeit",
        "en": "The sixteen steps of mindfulness of breathing"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Recorded at the weakest strength on purpose. The discourse sets out four tetrads of four steps and assigns them to the four foundations of mindfulness. It presents a progression; it does not say that a step is unreachable without the one before it, and the question whether the order is binding or expository is contested among practitioners to this day."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmp:Anapanasati"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:presentationOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmp:Anapanasati"
      },
      "source": "Majjhima Nikāya 118",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Theravada"
    },
    {
      "dcterms:date": "1832",
      "definition": {
        "en": "The English naming of the dakṣiṇācāra and vāmācāra divisions of the Śākta sect as the followers of a right hand and a left hand ritual, together with the characterisation of the second as impure, as running contrary to what is usual, and as something its adherents will not publicly avow."
      },
      "id": "pmc:RightAndLeftHandNomenclature",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Wiedergabe der beiden Śākta-Zweige als rechtshändiges und linkshändiges Ritual",
        "en": "The rendering of the two Śākta branches as a right and a left hand ritual"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Held as an ordering act because the naming is regularly credited to Blavatsky, who wrote in 1877 and in 1888. It stands in Wilson forty-five years earlier, in the volume of the Asiatic Researches for 1832, in those terms and with the moral charge already attached. Whoever first coined it, it was not she, and a record that repeats the later attribution reports a reception as an origin. See prima-materia#57.",
          "No pm:ordersClaim is given, because the division it orders is not yet in the graph as a claim of its own: that would need a passage in a tantra rather than a passage in Wilson. See prima-materia#53.",
          "The same section carries a ranking the naming does not survive. Wilson translates a passage he ascribes to the Kulārṇava that grades the Veda lowest and ascends through the Vaiṣṇava and the Śaiva to the right hand Śākta, then sets the left hand above the right, the Siddhānta above that, and the Kaula above all. In the material being ordered, the left is therefore the higher of the two; the English rendering keeps the pair and reverses the valuation. The verse is not located in an edition of the Kulārṇava here and is not held as a claim of that work. See prima-materia#56."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:compiledBy": "Horace Hayman Wilson",
      "source": "H. H. Wilson, Sketch of the Religious Sects of the Hindus, Asiatic Researches XVII (Calcutta 1832), the sections headed Dakshinas, or Bhaktas and Vamis, or Vamacharis",
      "type": "pm:Systematizing"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:KasdejaTeachesStrikingOfSpirits",
      "label": {
        "de": "Kasdeja offenbart das Schlagen der Geister",
        "en": "Kasdeja discloses the striking of spirits"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Book of Parables; no Aramaic fragment of chapters 37-71 is attested at Qumran."
      },
      "pm:ascribedCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:StrikingOfSpirits"
      },
      "pm:ascribedTo": {
        "id": "pmc:Kasdeja"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": [
        "1 Enoch 69:12 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
        "J.T. Milik, The Books of Enoch: Aramaic Fragments of Qumrân Cave 4 (Oxford, 1976)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Attributing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A cross-tradition structural analogue, weaker than owl:sameAs and distinct from skos:closeMatch in that it asserts role-in-process similarity rather than lexical overlap."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "id": "pm:analogueConcept",
      "label": {
        "en": "analogue concept"
      },
      "range": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The practice a yielding claim proceeds from."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Yielding",
      "id": "pm:byPractice",
      "label": {
        "de": "durch Praxis",
        "en": "by practice"
      },
      "range": "pm:Practicing",
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A mixture of air and fire that pervades bodies and, by its tension, holds them in cohesion; a principle of the world rather than a capacity of a practitioner."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Pneuma",
      "label": {
        "de": "Pneuma",
        "en": "Pneuma"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The associated doctrines are that of tension and that of total mixture, by which two bodies occupy the same place throughout."
      },
      "source": "Chrysippus, in Galen, De placitis Hippocratis et Platonis V.3.8",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:StoicPhysics"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Vital breath, presented as that on which the parts of the person rest and out of which beings come forth, in the image of a wheel resting on its hub."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Prana",
      "label": {
        "de": "Prāṇa",
        "en": "Prāṇa"
      },
      "source": [
        "Chāndogya Upaniṣad 1.11.5",
        "Chāndogya Upaniṣad 7.15.1 (Sanskrit; received prapāṭhaka-khaṇḍa numbering)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Upanisadic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that in the present age success is attained by Kaula worship alone, and that the mantra received from a Kaula brings knowledge of Brahman, where the mantra received from one of the lower dispositions reproduces that disposition in the receiver."
      },
      "id": "pmc:KaulaAboveTheOtherWays",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die kaulische Verehrung als der in diesem Zeitalter tragende Weg",
        "en": "Kaula worship as the way that succeeds in this age"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Kaula is set above the other ways here, not beside them. That is the same order Wilson translates from a passage he ascribes to the Kulārṇava, in which the left hand stands above the right and Kaula above all — an order the Western pair of names keeps while reversing its valuation. Two witnesses, one of them located by chapter and verse in a named translation and one not; the second is still to be found. See prima-materia#56."
      },
      "source": [
        "Mahānirvāṇa Tantra IV.20 (trans. A. Avalon, Tantra of the Great Liberation, London 1913)",
        "Mahānirvāṇa Tantra XIV.203 (trans. A. Avalon, Tantra of the Great Liberation, London 1913)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SaivaSakta"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A disagreement about what becomes of the bounded self at the end of the path, carried on inside the modern occult literature with the same term on both sides. One party holds that a self which is not surrendered ossifies and is consumed by time, and calls that the second death; the other holds that a self brought forth and made to endure is the attainment, and names it with a word of self-generation."
      },
      "id": "pmc:WhetherTheSelfIsKeptOrSurrendered",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ob das Bewahren des Selbst das Ziel des Weges ist oder sein Scheitern",
        "en": "Whether keeping the self is the goal of the path or its failure"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "One asymmetry the record should not smooth. The earlier side values the matter from a unitive end and treats the surviving self as a remainder that time consumes; the later values it from a separative end and treats the same survival as the work itself. Neither is a misreading of the other. A continuity story running from the one to the other as a single tradition with a changing mood reports a break as a development.",
          "That is why the disagreement is worth a node. Read as a quarrel about practices it dissolves into nothing, because neither side defines the path by practices. Read as a quarrel about the end state it is sharp and not decidable from inside either work, because each describes the same movement and calls it by the opposite name. See prima-materia#61 and prima-materia#68.",
          "The sides do not differ over means, and both say so. The one states that the signpost of a right-hand and a left-hand path is barely legible, that the two look alike for a long way, and that the question is at which point they diverge; he puts the divergence at the crossing of the Abyss and separates the sorcerer from the brother of the left-hand path as sharply as a thief from a tyrant. The other derives his word from an Egyptian term for coming into being and applies it to the transformation of the will, not to any particular practice. Whatever is at issue between them, it is not method."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:disputedClaim": {
        "id": "pmc:XeperAsSelfGeneration"
      },
      "source": [
        "A. Crowley, Magick Without Tears, chapter XII, on the left-hand path and the black brothers (electronic text of the collected letters)",
        "M. A. Aquino, The Temple of Set (Temple of Set, San Francisco), in the account of the Word Xeper and its Egyptian derivation"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Disputing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Shield, breastplate and the sword for slaughter, disclosed as instruments of killing rather than as craft."
      },
      "id": "pmc:WeaponsOfDeath",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Waffen des Todes",
        "en": "The weapons of death"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 69:6 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A Watcher whose teaching concerns the moon; the Ethiopic gives its course, the Aramaic-Greek witness gives its signs under the name Sahriel."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Sariel",
      "label": {
        "de": "Sariel",
        "en": "Sariel"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:HathaChapterOrder",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Handbuch ordnet Haltung, Atem, Siegel und Versenkung",
        "en": "The manual orders posture, breath, seal and absorption"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Recorded at the weakest strength on purpose. The four chapters run posture, breath, seal, absorption, and that arrangement is often cited as though it were itself a rule. The rule is in II.1; the chapter order is only an arrangement."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:NadiPurification"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
