      },
      "source": "John Cassian, Collationes II (De discretione)",
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ChristianAsceticDiscernment"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The reading of import from terrestrial phenomena."
      },
      "id": "pmc:SignsOfTheEarth",
      "label": {
        "de": "Zeichen der Erde",
        "en": "Signs of the earth"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Byzantine tradition of the prayer of the heart and of the vision of the uncreated light, defended in the fourteenth century against the objection that such a light must be created."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Hesychasm",
      "label": {
        "de": "Hesychasmus",
        "en": "Hesychasm"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. For every citation in this file the edition or translation the passage was read from is not recoverable, and it is left unstated rather than guessed. The Philokalia exists in Greek and in several translations whose contents and numbering differ, so a citation naming only the volume does not yet locate a text."
      },
      "source": "Gregory Palamas, Triads in Defence of the Holy Hesychasts",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A conclusion drawn by an editor or compiler that the source does not state."
      },
      "id": "pm:compilerInference",
      "label": {
        "de": "Schluss des Bearbeiters",
        "en": "Compiler inference"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Admissible as attestation of an ordering act, never of the claim being ordered. Enforced by pm:AttributionShape."
      },
      "type": "pm:Attesting"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:LovePresupposesApatheia",
      "label": {
        "de": "Liebe setzt Apatheia voraus, Erkenntnis die Liebe",
        "en": "Love presupposes apatheia, and knowledge presupposes love"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The chain is stated as generation and not as sequence: each term is the child or the flower of the one before it. That is why the strength here is necessity, and why a consequence can be quoted at all."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:consequenceOfSkipping": "Love is called the offspring of apatheia and is said to keep the door to the contemplation of created things, so that without it the door is not passed.",
      "pm:dependentStep": [
        {
          "id": "pmc:Agape"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:Physike"
        }
      ],
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:statedNecessity"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": [
        {
          "id": "pmc:Agape"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:Apatheia"
        }
      ],
      "source": [
        "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos 81",
        "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos 84"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:DesertMonasticism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Who devised or carried the protocol. A protocol without a named author is unaccountable, and an unaccountable protocol is not evidence of anything."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Testing",
      "id": "pm:examinedBy",
      "label": {
        "de": "geprüft von",
        "en": "examined by"
      },
      "type": "owl:DatatypeProperty"
    },
    {
      "dcterms:date": "20th century",
      "definition": {
        "en": "The reading of the eight limbs as a ladder that must be climbed in order, each limb a precondition of the next."
      },
      "id": "pmc:SerialReadingOfTheEightLimbs",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die serielle Lesart der acht Glieder",
        "en": "The serial reading of the eight limbs"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The inner and outer categories come from the commentarial layer, and the hardening of the ranking into a rule of practice belongs to twentieth-century yoga pedagogy. Recorded here so that the reading is available as what it is. The base text at II.29 enumerates and does not order."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:compiledBy": "The commentarial tradition, sharpened in twentieth-century yoga pedagogy",
      "pm:ordersClaim": {
        "id": "pmc:AstangaLimbs"
      },
      "source": "Patañjali, Yogasūtra II.29",
      "type": "pm:Systematizing"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:IddhiPresupposesFourthJhana",
      "label": {
        "de": "Übernatürliche Kraft setzt die vierte Vertiefung voraus",
        "en": "Supernormal power presupposes the fourth absorption"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The handbook assigns the task to a meditator who has reached the fourth absorption and calls that absorption the natural plane for obtaining the power. It does not, at these places, say what happens to one who attempts it from elsewhere."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:Iddhividha"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:FourthJhana"
      },
      "source": [
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.1 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.12-13 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Theravada"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A disagreement about whether the eight limbs form a sequence that must be climbed. The sūtra that names them enumerates and says nothing about order; the commentarial layer introduces the categories of outer and inner limb, and later teaching hardens these into a rule of practice."
      },
      "id": "pmc:LimbOrderDispute",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ob die Glieder der Reihe nach zu durchlaufen sind",
        "en": "Whether the limbs must be traversed in order"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The disagreement is not between two traditions but between a base text and its readers, which is the commonest shape a dispute takes in this collection and the easiest to lose, because the readers usually win by default."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:disputedClaim": {
        "id": "pmc:AstangaLimbs"
      },
      "source": [
        "Patañjali, Yogasūtra II.29",
        "Vyāsa, Yogabhāṣya on Yogasūtra II.29"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Disputing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The source instructs that one comes before the other, without saying what follows from disregarding it."
      },
      "id": "pm:prescribedOrder",
      "label": {
        "de": "Angeordnete Reihenfolge",
        "en": "Prescribed order"
      },
      "type": "pm:PrerequisiteStrength"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Breath or spirit, named as the first of the ten emanations, from which air issues, from air water, and from water fire."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Ruach",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ruach",
        "en": "Ruaḥ"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The citation was extended from 1:9-10 to 1:9-12 when the passage was reopened: in this numbering the first emanation is breath, the second breath from breath, and only the third and fourth carry water from breath and fire from water. The earlier range stopped two verses short of the sequence the definition states. The numbering is recension-dependent throughout, and the long and Saadia versions divide the same material otherwise.",
          "The text also has the patriarch stirring the letters with breath, which is the closest the source comes to breath as an operative medium; it remains an act of formation, not a conduction through the body."
        ]
      },
      "source": [
        "Sefer Yetzirah 1:9-12 (Short Version; numbering of Kaplan 1997)",
        "Sefer Yetzirah 6:7 (Short Version; numbering of Kaplan 1997)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SeferYetzirah"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A retention in which the breath is drawn in over the tongue, protruded past the lips, and released through the nostrils."
      },
      "id": "pmp:Sitali",
      "label": {
        "de": "Śītalī",
        "en": "Śītalī"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.57-58 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hathayoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "An experiential account given in the first person by the one who had it, recorded once."
      },
      "id": "pm:firstPersonReport",
      "label": {
        "de": "Erfahrungsbericht",
        "en": "First-person report"
      },
      "type": "pm:Attesting"
    },
    {
      "dcterms:date": "2004",
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that emotions form a graded ladder that indicates how near a person stands to a desired state, with joy, knowledge, empowerment, freedom, love and appreciation at its top rung."
      },
      "id": "pmc:HicksEmotionalGuidanceScale",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Skala der emotionalen Führung",
        "en": "The emotional guidance scale"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The upper block of this scale reappears almost word for word in later lists that present themselves as older. Recorded so that the wording has a dated first appearance to be checked against."
      },
      "pm:circulatesAs": "A law of the emotions, presented as a description of how feeling works rather than as one author's arrangement.",
      "pm:originatedBy": "Esther Hicks and Jerry Hicks",
      "source": "E. Hicks and J. Hicks, Ask and It Is Given (2004), ch. 22",
      "type": "pm:Originating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A retention in which the mouth is held closed and the breath is drawn through both nostrils so that it grazes the throat and sounds as it passes."
      },
      "id": "pmp:Ujjayi",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ujjāyī",
        "en": "Ujjāyī"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.51-53 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hathayoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Freedom from the disturbance of the passions, called the flower of ascetic labour."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Apatheia",
      "label": {
        "de": "Apatheia",
        "en": "Apatheia"
      },
      "source": "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos 81",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:DesertMonasticism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A seal made by pressing the perineum with the heel and contracting the anus, by which the downward wind is drawn upward."
      },
      "id": "pmp:Mulabandha",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Wurzelsiegel",
        "en": "The root seal"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā III.61-63 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hathayoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A dream held to come from a god rather than from the sleeper, and distinguished from the ordinary dream not by its content but by the state in which it arrives."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Theopemptos",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der gottgesandte Traum",
        "en": "The god-sent dream"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The state is described as the one in which sleep is departing and waking beginning, or as a condition between the two, accompanied by a voice, by a light, and by an inability to move the eyes. This is the closest description in the collection to what modern accounts call the hypnopompic state, and the immobility is named as part of the sign rather than as an obstacle. What arrives in it is a communication received, not a faculty exercised."
      },
      "source": "Iamblichus, De mysteriis III.2-3 (ed. des Places 1966)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:NeoplatonicTheurgy"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A leader among the Watchers, named in the Book of the Watchers as the one who disclosed metallurgy, weaponry and adornment."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Azazel",
      "label": {
        "de": "Azazel",
        "en": "Azazel"
      },
      "skos:altLabel": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Asael"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:1 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Two traditions assign direction inside the body to something other than inhaling and exhaling. One assigns it to two winds driven against each other in a single operation; the other to a place in a graded sequence, with descent in the middle of it and ascent at its end. Neither pairs a direction with a phase of the breath."
      },
      "id": "pmc:DirectionIsNotThePhaseOfBreathing",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Richtung trägt eine gestufte Bahn, nicht die Atemphase",
        "en": "Direction is carried by a graded course, not by the phase of breathing"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "A second correspondence sits alongside this one and is deliberately not asserted as a separate node yet: both traditions sanction the wrong handling of the breath with death, the one saying that the breath kills the practitioner who forces it, the other that going against the sequence is to die. Two death sanctions are a thinner finding than they look, since a warning of death is the commonest form a strong prohibition takes anywhere, and the two do not agree on what the fault is: one names haste and force, the other names reversal of order. Recorded here so that the next reader can weigh it rather than rediscover it.",
          "The independence is asymmetric and the node says so rather than claiming more than it has. The jade inscription is dated to the fourth or third century before the common era on the reading this collection follows, which is earlier than any documented transmission of Indian breath technique into China; for that witness there is no route of contact to be had. The manual is fifteenth-century and stands downstream of centuries of Buddhist traffic in both directions, so its side of the correspondence cannot be declared contact-free with the same confidence. What the two share is therefore better read as one early independent witness and one late witness of uncertain independence, which is weaker than a convergence of two early ones and stronger than a citation chain."
        ]
      },
      "pm:convergingConcept": [
        {
          "id": "pmc:OpposedDirectionsOfPranaAndApana"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:XingqiDescent"
        }
      ],
      "pm:independentAttestation": [
        {
          "id": "pmt:Daoist"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmt:Hathayoga"
        }
      ],
      "source": [
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.47 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
        "Xingqi yupei ming, in D. Harper, Early Chinese Medical Literature (1998), p. 125"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Converging"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Hearing beyond the range of the physical ear."
      },
      "id": "pmc:DivyaSrotra",
      "label": {
        "de": "Göttliches Ohr",
        "en": "Divine ear"
      },
      "source": "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.41",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:PatanjalaYoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
